---
// Alexandria.astro - Main landing page sections
// v2.2 outline: Future, Bridging the gap, The substrate, The near future,
//               What runs today, Tomorrow, Us, Invitation
import '../styles/alexandria.css';
---

<!-- ==================== SEGMENT 1: FUTURE ==================== -->
<section class="hero" id="hero">
  <div class="hero-image-container">
    <img
      src="/hero.jpg"
      alt="A futuristic vision of the ancient Library of Alexandria"
      class="hero-image"
    />
    <div class="hero-image-fade"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="container hero-text">
    <h1>AI should elevate humanity, not the other way around.</h1>
    <p class="hero-vision">
      We envision small teams of centered, rested people directing large teams of AI colleagues.
      Shipping at the speed of imagination.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-divider"></div>

<!-- ==================== SEGMENT 2: BRIDGING THE GAP ==================== -->
<section class="bridge" id="bridge">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="bridge-image">
      <img
        src="/bridge.png"
        alt="An ancient-technofuturistic bridge over water with luminous-sailed ships passing through its arches"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <h2>Bridging the almost-there gap.</h2>
    <p>AI is staggering right now. It writes, it codes, it reasons.</p>
    <p>
      AI is maddening right now. It lives in a thousand open tabs. It forgets between sessions. It
      works one-on-one when the work is many-to-many.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bridging the gap between almost-there and actually there takes a substrate. An operating
      system for AI-native companies.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-divider"></div>

<!-- ==================== SEGMENT 3: THE SUBSTRATE ==================== -->
<section class="substrate" id="substrate">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="substrate-image">
      <img
        src="/stack-library.png"
        alt="Ancient library with glowing crystals and circuit-like floor channels"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <h2>The substrate.</h2>
    <p>
      AI-native organizations need primitives that can answer three questions, 24/7: What's true?
      What to do? What happened?
    </p>

    <div class="substrate-trio">
      <div class="substrate-piece">
        <h3>A library of what is true.</h3>
        <p>
          Structured, linked, queryable. Strategy, brand, customers, products, research &mdash;
          every dimension every agent and human needs, in one canonical place.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="substrate-piece">
        <h3>A playbook of what to do when.</h3>
        <p>
          Triggers, rules, plays. The decisions you've already made, encoded so an agent team can
          act on them at any hour without paging you. NASA has done this for decades.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="substrate-piece">
        <h3>A ledger of what happened.</h3>
        <p>
          An append-only record of every action, decision, handoff, approval. New agents pick up
          work by reading it. So do humans. The Navy has done this for centuries.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-divider"></div>

<!-- ==================== SEGMENT 4: THE NEAR FUTURE ==================== -->
<section class="near-future" id="near-future">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="near-future-image">
      <img
        src="/stack-chess.png"
        alt="Hellenofuturistic chessboard with classical statue pieces and holographic overlays"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <h2>The near future, as we're living it.</h2>
    <p>We've been running an AI software factory for most of 2026.</p>
    <p>
      Agents are becoming colleagues. Clear roles, decision authority, just-in-time context.
    </p>
    <p>
      Creative and design work still takes the time it takes. But execution is moving to AI at
      factory speed in the domains that compile: Software, writing, visuals, research print
      overnight for morning review.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our old processes and tools are collapsing under the strain of the speed and volume. Sprints,
      tickets, dashboards, inbox-driven coordination, suck now. We often go home feeling like we've
      been in a car accident.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-divider"></div>

<!-- ==================== SEGMENT 5: WHAT RUNS TODAY ==================== -->
<section class="runs-today" id="runs-today">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Where the work is.</h2>
    <p class="runs-today-intro">Two projects in alpha, ready for first hands.</p>

    <div class="runs-today-grid">
      <div class="runs-today-card">
        <div class="runs-today-card-image product-screenshot">
          <img
            src="/raven-screenshot.png"
            alt="A Raven library session showing assessment of gap areas and source material"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <div class="runs-today-card-body">
          <p class="runs-today-eyebrow"><em>Proof of concept.</em></p>
          <p>
            One agent, one job, the first proof of an AI teammate. Raven is your senior product
            manager. She holds the canonical library of product context &mdash; your specs, decision
            threads, all queryable from one place &mdash; and uses it to make you look good as a
            project owner: sharpening what you ship so downstream agents aren't guessing, surfacing
            the questions only you can answer before you open the day.
          </p>
          <p>
            She lives on Claude. Setup is from a terminal and you do it on a call with us so we see
            what breaks. Honestly: she's good at the job and rough in real ways.
          </p>
          <a href="/onboarding" class="runs-today-link">Become an Alexandrian &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="runs-today-card">
        <div class="runs-today-card-image book-cover">
          <a href="/book">
            <img src="/book-cover.png" alt="The 7 Turn Work Week book cover" loading="lazy" />
          </a>
        </div>
        <div class="runs-today-card-body">
          <p class="runs-today-eyebrow"><em>Field manual.</em></p>
          <p>
            The 7 Turn Work Week. Running our company factory-style broke me in under ten days. I
            went home thinking I had COVID and slept the weekend away. I didn't have COVID &mdash; I
            had an overloaded, abused brain. Every developer I talked to had a softer version of the
            same horror story.
          </p>
          <p>
            So I went deep on human capacity and on the protocols for automated work versus batch
            work. Alexandria is the long-term answer. It's not ready.
          </p>
          <p>
            This &ldquo;book&rdquo; is the triage journal in the meantime. I've built a bespoke
            capacity monitor and broken my weeks into turns and phases, Magic: The Gathering style.
            Alpha readers send feedback on the writing, their attempts at the recommendations, and
            their own inventions for making AI-scale work more humane.
          </p>
          <a href="/book" class="runs-today-link">Become an Alpha Reader &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-divider"></div>

<!-- ==================== SEGMENT 6: TOMORROW ==================== -->
<section class="tomorrow" id="tomorrow">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="tomorrow-image">
      <img
        src="/roadmap-city.png"
        alt="Three-panel city progression from ancient village to classical city to futuristic empire"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <h2>The arc.</h2>

    <div class="roadmap-tiers">
      <div class="tier active">
        <div class="tier-label">
          Now
          <span class="tier-time">2026</span>
        </div>
        <div class="tier-content">
          <h3>One agent, real load.</h3>
          <p>
            Software factories are hitting the context wall first &mdash; that's where Raven goes
            in. We're hardening her against real codebases this year. If you're running multi-agent
            development, or you're an operator with edge using AI to do what used to take a team,
            this is where you come in.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tier-cta">
          <a href="/onboarding" class="btn-outline">Try it now</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tier">
        <div class="tier-label">
          Next
          <span class="tier-time">2027</span>
        </div>
        <div class="tier-content">
          <h3>The whole company runs on Alexandria.</h3>
          <p>
            The same primitives spread out of engineering. Design, marketing, ops &mdash; every
            function that's now operating at factory pace needs the same canonical context, the same
            encoded judgment, the same record. Alexandria becomes the nervous system. The cockpit
            ships in here.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tier-cta">
          <a href="#invitation" class="btn-outline">Follow along</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tier">
        <div class="tier-label">
          Later
          <span class="tier-time">2028</span>
        </div>
        <div class="tier-content">
          <h3>Federated.</h3>
          <p>
            Company to division to team, all reading from one substrate, all writing to one record.
            Model-agnostic. The senior PM agent from the top of this page isn't a tool anymore
            &mdash; she's a layer of organizational intelligence that gets sharper every action it
            takes. Alexandria becomes as foundational as cloud compute.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tier-cta">
          <a href="#invitation" class="btn-outline">Follow along</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-divider"></div>

<!-- ==================== SEGMENT 7: TEAM ==================== -->
<section class="team" id="team">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>The Team</h2>
    <div class="team-grid">
      <div class="team-member">
        <h3>Danvers Fleury</h3>
        <p class="role">Architect of Alexandria</p>
        <p>
          Started at Monster.com weeks after its IPO, fell in love with the problem of how teams
          scale and never stopped working it. 25 years across startups, turnarounds, and
          organizational design.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="team-member">
        <h3>Jess Martin</h3>
        <p class="role">Builder of Alexandria</p>
        <p>
          Recruited out of school to build The Sims. Two decades building knowledge representation
          and real-time collaboration systems across multiple startups.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-divider"></div>

<!-- ==================== SEGMENT 8: INVITATION ==================== -->
<section class="invitation" id="invitation">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="invitation-image">
      <img
        src="/hero-mosaic.png"
        alt="Ancient mosaic fresco of diverse people gathered around a holographic world map"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <h2>Come build with us.</h2>
    <p>If you've read this far, you're probably one of two kinds of builder.</p>
    <p>
      The first runs a software factory and has hit the wall of being the coordination layer. The
      second is a business operator with an edge &mdash; domain knowledge, market access, a
      strategic read &mdash; who wants to build an AI team, go forth, and prosper.
    </p>
    <p>
      If that's you, become an Alexandrian. Forty-five minutes on a video call with one or both of
      us. The Alexandrian Agreement is here, so you know exactly how we work before you book.
    </p>

    <div class="invitation-ctas">
      <a href="/onboarding" class="btn btn-primary">Become an Alexandrian</a>
      <a href="/agreement" class="btn btn-outline">Read the Agreement</a>
    </div>

    <p class="invitation-follow">If you want to follow without installing yet:</p>
    <ul class="invitation-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Read along.</strong>
        <a href="https://sociotechnica.org/notebook/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Labnotes at sociotechnica.org</a
        >, weekly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Read the book as it's written.</strong>
        <a href="/book">Become an Alpha Reader</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Watch the substrate evolve.</strong>
        <a
          href="https://github.com/GetAlexandria/alexandria"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Alexandria on GitHub</a
        >.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-divider"></div>

<!-- ==================== STAY IN THE LOOP ==================== -->
<section class="cta" id="cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Stay in the loop.</h2>
    <p>
      Labnotes weekly. Book chapters as they ship. Product updates when they matter. One list, one
      email.
    </p>
    <form
      class="email-form"
      action="https://buttondown.com/api/emails/embed-subscribe/sociotechnica"
      method="post"
      target="popupwindow"
    >
      <input type="email" name="email" placeholder="you@company.com" required />
      <input type="hidden" name="tag" value="alexandria" />
      <button type="submit">Subscribe</button>
    </form>
  </div>
</section>
